(ns face-liveness.ports
  "The camera/CV seam. face-liveness.core never touches an image, a video
   stream, or a camera API -- only face-liveness.model's named-point
   landmark maps, produced somewhere upstream of this protocol. Mirrors
   mrz.ports/IZoneOcr's split (this repo's sibling in the eKYC/AML effort:
   kotoba-lang/mrz, ADR-2607198200) and kotoba-lang's house host-injection
   convention generally (num.protocol/IBackend, aml.ports/IAmlScreening).

   NO IMPLEMENTATION SHIPS IN THIS REPO. A real ILandmarkTracker needs an
   actual face-landmark detection model (classical cascade/regression or a
   small neural net) -- the org's stated ML stance (kotoba-lang/inference's
   README) is torch+num, not ONNX/TF.js/WebNN, and there is no vision-
   workload precedent in torch+num today (LLM-shaped only). Shipping an
   unvalidated home-grown landmark detector here would be the exact
   false-confidence failure mode this whole eKYC/AML effort exists to
   avoid -- see this repo's README.md.")

(defprotocol ILandmarkTracker
  (track-frame [tracker image-data at]
    "`image-data` is opaque to this protocol (a real implementation defines
     its own shape -- raw pixels, a decoded frame object, whatever its
     detector needs). Returns a face-liveness.model/frame ({:frame/at
     :frame/landmarks {...named points...}}), or nil if no face was
     detected in this frame. A tracker that can only find SOME of the named
     points a challenge type needs should still return what it found —
     face-liveness.geometry's functions already propagate nil for missing
     points rather than guessing."))
